  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "e/6QYsB7cQv02ouKseWHCZHMbSI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:37:36.191Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Authentication without a round trip\n\n`ph access-token` turns the CLI's cryptographic identity into a bearer token for Powerhouse APIs (reactor-api / Switchboard). It signs a verifiable credential with the keypair `ph login` created and wraps it as a JWT. **No network request is made** \u2014 tokens are generated entirely locally, so this works offline and cannot be rate-limited or fail on a Renown outage.\n\nPrerequisite: run `ph login` first, which generates the keypair at `.ph/.keypair.json` and optionally links an Ethereum address, stored at `.ph/.renown.json`.\n\nThe JWT carries `iss` and `sub` set to the CLI's `did:key:...`, a credential subject with chain id, network id and the linked address if authenticated, `exp` from `--expiry` (default `7d`, accepting `7d` / `24h` / `3600` / `3600s`), and `aud` when `--audience <url>` is passed.\n\n## The stdout/stderr split is the API\n\nToken information (DID, address, expiry) goes to **stderr**; the token alone goes to **stdout**. That is what makes command substitution safe:\n\n```bash\nTOKEN=$(ph access-token --expiry 1d)\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:4001/graphql \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -d '{\"query\": \"{ drives { id name } }\"}'\n\nexport PH_ACCESS_TOKEN=$(ph access-token)\n```\n\nYou see the diagnostics on your terminal while the variable captures only the token \u2014 no trimming, no parsing.\n\n## Two conditions on the far end\n\nBecause the token is **self-signed with your own private key**, it proves only that the holder controls that DID. It carries no authority by itself: the recipient API must independently *trust* the CLI's DID to accept it. And for reactor-api, authentication is only enforced when `AUTH_ENABLED=true` \u2014 with auth disabled the token is simply ignored, which is a common source of \"my token isn't working\" confusion running in the opposite direction.\n\nRelated: `ph registry-login` performs the analogous mint against a package registry and writes the resulting Renown bearer token into `~/.npmrc`.\n\n_Source: `clis/ph-cli/src/commands/access-token.ts`, mirrored in `apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/07-CLITooling/00-PowerhouseCLI.md`, Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:37:35.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:37:36.191Z"
    }
  },
